# Source: Exercise list "ICT3101 Exercises - Deductive Verification", problem 5 b), with its solution sheet.
id: multiply-by-adding
title: Multiplication by repeated addition
topic: Loops
level: 2
source: Exercise list "HoareLogic Exercises", problem 5 b
tags: while, invariant, bounds

task:
  Give a proof outline and a loop invariant.

pre: x = x' && y = y' && y >= 0
post: z = x * y

program:
  {P}
  {R4}
  z := 0;            // i1
  {R3}
  a := 1;            // i2
  {I}
  while (a <= y) do {
    {I && a <= y}
    {R2}
    z := z + x;      // b1
    {R1}
    a := a + 1       // b2
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(a <= y)}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: Find a candidate invariant
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  The counter $a$ starts at $1$, so after $a - 1$ iterations $z$ holds
  $a - 1$ copies of $x$. The loop stops when $a = y + 1$. The solution
  sheet also keeps $x = x'$, which is true but not needed, since $x$ is
  never assigned.
set: I = z = x * (a - 1) && 1 <= a <= y + 1

step: Establishment
rule: ASSIGN, SEQ, WP
focus: i1, i2
calc:
  wp(a := 1, I)
  = z = x * (1 - 1) && 1 <= 1 <= y + 1
set: R3 = z = x * (1 - 1) && 1 <= 1 <= y + 1
calc:
  wp(z := 0, R3)
  = 0 = x * (1 - 1) && 1 <= 1 <= y + 1
  = 0 <= y
set: R4 = 0 <= y
vc: x = x' && y = y' && y >= 0 ==> 0 <= y

step: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, SEQ, CONSEQUENCE
focus: b1, b2
calc:
  wp(a := a + 1, I)
  = z = x * (a + 1 - 1) && 1 <= a + 1 <= y + 1
  = z = x * a && 0 <= a <= y
set: R1 = z = x * a && 0 <= a <= y
calc:
  wp(z := z + x, R1)
  = z + x = x * a && 0 <= a <= y
set: R2 = z + x = x * a && 0 <= a <= y
vc: z = x * (a - 1) && 1 <= a <= y + 1 && a <= y ==> z + x = x * a && 0 <= a <= y
why:
  $z + x = x * (a - 1) + x = x * a$. The bounds $1 <= a$ and $a <= y$ give
  $0 <= a <= y$.

step: The postcondition at the exit
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
vc: z = x * (a - 1) && 1 <= a <= y + 1 && !(a <= y) ==> z = x * y
why:
  $a > y$ and $a <= y + 1$ give $a = y + 1$, so $z = x * (y + 1 - 1) = x * y$.
result: The triple is valid. A variant for termination is $y + 1 - a$.
